1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:06,727
Right now, the monthly mortgage payment
on a median-priced American home

2
00:00:06,727 --> 00:00:07,612
is $2,243.

3
00:00:07,932 --> 00:00:13,492
And the typical rent on a
single-family house? $2,243.

4
00:00:13,812 --> 00:00:15,441
The same number. To the dollar.

5
00:00:15,761 --> 00:00:19,787
So buying costs the same as renting,
except you build equity. Easy

6
00:00:19,787 --> 00:00:20,763
decision, right?

7
00:00:21,663 --> 00:00:26,008
Not even close. We built a model of
both choices with this month's real

8
00:00:26,008 --> 00:00:30,231
data, and under typical assumptions,
the renter stays richer than the

9
00:00:30,231 --> 00:00:31,822
buyer for over nine years.

10
00:00:32,142 --> 00:00:35,574
But change one habit, and that
number collapses to about two.

11
00:00:36,474 --> 00:00:40,200
By the end of this video, you'll know
the three numbers that decide your

12
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:42,735
own breakeven year, and
how to run them yourself.

13
00:00:46,235 --> 00:00:49,880
When people compare renting to
buying, they put the rent next to the

14
00:00:49,880 --> 00:00:52,452
mortgage payment. That's
the tip of the iceberg.

15
00:00:52,772 --> 00:00:56,193
Here's the full cost of owning
that median home, in month one.

16
00:00:56,513 --> 00:00:59,755
The mortgage: $2,243.

17
00:01:00,075 --> 00:01:06,286
Property tax, at the national
average effective rate of about 0.9%:

18
00:01:06,286 --> 00:01:07,491
another $313.

19
00:01:07,811 --> 00:01:13,963
Homeowners insurance, now averaging
around $3,000 a year: $251.

20
00:01:14,283 --> 00:01:20,023
And maintenance: roofs, water heaters,
the things nobody budgets for. Even

21
00:01:20,023 --> 00:01:25,531
at a conservative 1% of the home's
value per year, that's $347 a month.

22
00:01:25,851 --> 00:01:33,462
Total: $3,153. That's $910 a month
more than renting a similar house.

23
00:01:34,362 --> 00:01:38,168
And of that first mortgage payment,
how much actually becomes equity?

24
00:01:38,488 --> 00:01:40,554
Just $267.

25
00:01:40,874 --> 00:01:47,282
The other $1,975 is interest. In
month one, 88 cents of every mortgage

26
00:01:47,282 --> 00:01:49,479
dollar goes to the bank.

27
00:01:50,379 --> 00:01:54,658
That's the key idea. Rent is money
you'll never see again. But so are

28
00:01:54,658 --> 00:01:57,759
interest, property tax,
insurance and maintenance.

29
00:01:58,079 --> 00:02:01,774
Owning doesn't stop your money
from disappearing. It just makes it

30
00:02:01,774 --> 00:02:03,174
disappear in more places.

31
00:02:06,674 --> 00:02:10,945
To make this a fair fight, we gave
two households exactly the same money.

32
00:02:11,265 --> 00:02:20,082
The buyer puts 20% down, $83,340,
plus about $8,300 in closing costs.

33
00:02:20,402 --> 00:02:26,832
The renter doesn't buy, so they
invest that same $91,674 instead.

34
00:02:27,152 --> 00:02:30,943
Then, every single month, whoever
has the cheaper housing invests the

35
00:02:30,943 --> 00:02:31,547
difference.

36
00:02:31,867 --> 00:02:36,973
Today that's the renter, by $910 a
month. But rent rises over time while

37
00:02:36,973 --> 00:02:41,087
the mortgage payment stays
fixed, so eventually, it flips.

38
00:02:41,987 --> 00:02:44,682
Here are the assumptions,
all from this year's data.

39
00:02:45,002 --> 00:02:49,539
Home values grow 4% a year, close
to their long-run national average.

40
00:02:49,859 --> 00:02:54,813
The renter's portfolio earns 7%,
deliberately below the stock market's

41
00:02:54,813 --> 00:02:57,006
roughly 10% historical average.

42
00:02:57,326 --> 00:03:01,203
When the buyer sells, they pay
about 6% in commissions and fees.

43
00:03:01,523 --> 00:03:04,769
And when the renter cashes out,
they pay capital gains tax.

44
00:03:05,089 --> 00:03:08,936
We left out the mortgage interest
deduction. It helps some buyers,

45
00:03:08,936 --> 00:03:13,133
especially single filers, and would
pull their breakeven earlier. That's

46
00:03:13,133 --> 00:03:15,465
one more reason to run your own numbers.

47
00:03:18,965 --> 00:03:22,719
Here's the net worth of both households,
meaning what each would walk away

48
00:03:22,719 --> 00:03:24,749
with if they sold everything that month.

49
00:03:25,069 --> 00:03:31,438
On day one, the buyer is already
behind. Closing costs are gone, and

50
00:03:31,438 --> 00:03:38,089
selling would cost another 6%. The
buyer starts with about $58,000. The

51
00:03:38,089 --> 00:03:39,588
renter, $91,674.

52
00:03:39,908 --> 00:03:44,321
After year one, the renter is
still ahead by about $31,000.

53
00:03:44,641 --> 00:03:48,592
Then the buyer starts closing the
gap. Every year the home appreciates,

54
00:03:48,592 --> 00:03:52,431
the loan shrinks, and rent creeps
up while the mortgage payment stays

55
00:03:52,431 --> 00:03:52,821
locked.

56
00:03:53,141 --> 00:03:55,692
And at 9.4 years, the lines cross.

57
00:03:56,592 --> 00:04:02,424
That's the breakeven. Sell before
it, and renting would have left you

58
00:04:02,424 --> 00:04:08,680
richer. Stay past it, and buying pulls
ahead, finishing thirty years about

59
00:04:08,680 --> 00:04:12,991
$143,000 richer: $1.3
million versus $1.15 million.

60
00:04:13,311 --> 00:04:18,429
Zillow's own national estimate, using
different assumptions, is 7.8 years.

61
00:04:18,429 --> 00:04:19,674
Same neighborhood.

62
00:04:19,993 --> 00:04:24,067
So the rule isn't that buying always
wins, or that renting is throwing

63
00:04:24,067 --> 00:04:27,384
money away. Buying is a bet
that you'll stay long enough.

64
00:04:30,884 --> 00:04:35,850
Now the honest part. 9.4 years is the
answer for one set of assumptions.

65
00:04:35,850 --> 00:04:39,162
Change any of three
numbers, and it moves a lot.

66
00:04:39,482 --> 00:04:45,331
Lever one: how fast the home appreciates.
At 4% a year, breakeven is 9.4

67
00:04:45,331 --> 00:04:45,818
years.

68
00:04:46,138 --> 00:04:48,793
At 5%, it drops to 4.6.

69
00:04:49,113 --> 00:04:52,911
But at 3%, buying doesn't catch
up within thirty years at all.

70
00:04:53,811 --> 00:04:59,894
Lever two: your mortgage rate. At
today's 7.11%, that's 9.4 years.

71
00:05:00,214 --> 00:05:02,144
At 6%, 4.8.

72
00:05:02,464 --> 00:05:04,420
At 5%, just over three.

73
00:05:04,740 --> 00:05:08,534
That's why a one-point drop in rates
matters far more than it sounds.

74
00:05:09,434 --> 00:05:14,367
Lever three is the big one: the
price-to-rent ratio. Take a home's price,

75
00:05:14,367 --> 00:05:19,367
and divide it by a year of rent on a
similar place. Nationally, right now,

76
00:05:19,367 --> 00:05:20,584
that's about 15.5.

77
00:05:20,904 --> 00:05:24,865
Where it's 12, meaning homes are
cheap relative to rent, buying breaks

78
00:05:24,865 --> 00:05:26,337
even in under three years.

79
00:05:26,657 --> 00:05:30,730
Where it's 18 or higher, at today's
rates, it doesn't break even within

80
00:05:30,730 --> 00:05:31,475
thirty years.

81
00:05:32,375 --> 00:05:36,602
Put two of those levers together and
you get the breakeven map. Mortgage

82
00:05:36,602 --> 00:05:39,771
rates run down the side,
price-to-rent across the top.

83
00:05:40,091 --> 00:05:43,836
Today's national numbers
land right here: 9.4 years.

84
00:05:44,156 --> 00:05:48,618
Lower rates or a lower price-to-rent,
and buying wins fast. Move a column

85
00:05:48,618 --> 00:05:53,018
or two to the right, into pricier
markets, and at today's rates it stops

86
00:05:53,018 --> 00:05:53,935
winning at all.

87
00:05:57,435 --> 00:06:01,876
Now, the twist. Our renter is extremely
disciplined. They invest every

88
00:06:01,876 --> 00:06:04,922
dollar they save, every
month, for thirty years.

89
00:06:05,242 --> 00:06:09,545
What if they don't? What if that
$910 a month just gets spent?

90
00:06:10,445 --> 00:06:14,497
Then buying breaks even in just
2.4 years, and never looks back.

91
00:06:14,817 --> 00:06:19,299
This is the real reason so many
homeowners end up wealthier than renters.

92
00:06:19,299 --> 00:06:22,124
It's not that a house
is a magical investment.

93
00:06:22,444 --> 00:06:26,548
It's that a mortgage is a forced
savings plan, and most of us are bad at

94
00:06:26,548 --> 00:06:27,574
forcing ourselves.

95
00:06:28,474 --> 00:06:32,182
One more honest caveat. Some of
what matters most doesn't fit in a

96
00:06:32,182 --> 00:06:32,857
spreadsheet.

97
00:06:33,177 --> 00:06:37,457
Renting buys flexibility. You can
move for a better job in weeks, not

98
00:06:37,457 --> 00:06:41,489
months, and if local prices fall,
that's your landlord's problem.

99
00:06:41,809 --> 00:06:46,075
Owning buys control. Nobody else
decides when you leave, what next year's

100
00:06:46,075 --> 00:06:48,529
rent will be, or what color the walls are.

101
00:06:48,849 --> 00:06:53,457
And owning concentrates risk. In our
model, nearly all of the buyer's net

102
00:06:53,457 --> 00:06:56,486
worth is one house, in
one town, under one roof.

103
00:06:56,806 --> 00:06:59,950
Put your own price on those, and
weigh them alongside the math.

104
00:07:03,450 --> 00:07:07,580
So, before you sign anything: how
long will you really stay? At today's

105
00:07:07,580 --> 00:07:11,884
national numbers, under about nine
years, renting tends to come out ahead.

106
00:07:12,204 --> 00:07:17,286
Find your price-to-rent ratio. At
today's rates, around 14 or lower breaks

107
00:07:17,286 --> 00:07:21,406
even in roughly five years. 18
or higher, not within thirty.

108
00:07:21,726 --> 00:07:26,952
Price in the full cost. Here, tax,
insurance and maintenance added 40.6%

109
00:07:26,952 --> 00:07:29,203
on top of the mortgage payment.

110
00:07:29,523 --> 00:07:33,361
And be honest about the habit. If
the difference won't get invested,

111
00:07:33,361 --> 00:07:34,716
buying wins much sooner.

112
